  "worked_solution": "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+\u0394{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $\u0394x$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of Euler's Method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=-5y,\\,y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{\u2032}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ Euler's method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot \u0394x.\n$$\n\nNow, let's check our statements:\n\n- Applying Euler's method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-5{y}_{n})\\cdot \u0394x \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-5{y}_{n}\\cdot \u0394x \\\\  & =(1-5\u0394x){y}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is false.\n\n- For stability, we require ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ Here, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n} & =(1-5\u0394x){y}_{n-1} \\\\  & =(1-5\u0394x)((1-5\u0394x){y}_{n-2}) \\\\  & =(1-5\u0394x)^{2}{y}_{n-2} \\\\  & \\,\\,\u22ee\\,\\,\u22ee \\\\  & =(1-5\u0394x)^{n}{y}_{0} \\\\  & =(1-5\u0394x)^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if $|1-5\u0394x|<1,$ as stated. Hence, statement II is true.\n\n- Expanding the inequality $|1-5\u0394x|<1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & <1-5\u0394x<1 \\\\ -2 & <-5\u0394x<0 \\\\ 0 & <5\u0394x<2 \\\\ 0 & <\u0394x<0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine stability for the given step sizes:\n\n- Since $\u0394x=0.2$ satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement III is true.\n\n- Since $\u0394x=0.25$ satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and III only\".",
